<SiteLayout title="Module 13: ICMP — fred.fish" description="ICMP reports network-layer errors and supports diagnostics. Ping and traceroute use ICMP behavior to help identify whether a destination or path is reachable." active="cisco">
<section class="source-lesson">
<div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
<header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 13" label="MODULE 13 / ICMP AND CONNECTIVITY TESTING" /><h1>Test the network.<br /><span>Interpret the response.</span></h1><p>ICMP reports network-layer errors and supports diagnostics. Ping and traceroute use ICMP behavior to help identify whether a destination or path is reachable.</p></header>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Understand ICMP messages</h2><p>ICMP for IPv4 and ICMPv6 for IPv6 carry control and error-reporting messages. Examples include destination unreachable and time exceeded. ICMP is not an application transport protocol like TCP or UDP; it supports IP operation and diagnostics.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1010 · IMG-1075</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Use ping to test reachability</h2><p>Ping sends an Echo Request and waits for an Echo Reply. A reply confirms that the destination responded to the probe at that time. A timeout can indicate a routing or addressing problem, but it can also mean that a firewall or host policy filters ICMP. Interpret ping alongside interface state and routing information.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1028 · IMG-1001</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Use traceroute to inspect a path</h2><p>Traceroute sends probes with progressively increasing TTL (IPv4) or Hop Limit (IPv6). Each router decrements the value; when it reaches zero, the router may return a Time Exceeded message. The sequence of responses helps reveal the path and where forwarding stops. Some hops may not respond because ICMP is filtered or rate-limited.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1028 · IMG-2271</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>ICMP</h2><p>Practice the key terms, procedures, and troubleshooting decisions from this module.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
</section>
</SiteLayout>
